WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.320
Boy meets girl, boy likes girl, boy fights evil&nbsp;
exes. Years after its release, the untold truth of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:11.440
"Scott Pilgrim vs. The World" further reinforces&nbsp;
what a one-of-a-kind movie it turned out to be.

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:16.000
Even with all the famous roles Michael&nbsp;
Cera has inhabited, it's still difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.800
to separate him from his performance as Scott&nbsp;
Pilgrim in "Scott Pilgrim vs. the World."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:25.600
Cera's distinctive film persona was just such&nbsp;
a perfect fit for the character, and his gift&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:30.480
for timing means that he's responsible for the&nbsp;
best line deliveries in the entire movie. Given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:35.280
how snugly he fits into this role, it shouldn't&nbsp;
be a surprise that director Edgar Wright always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:41.360
envisioned the part as being one that only Cera&nbsp;
could portray. As Wright told CinemaBlend in 2010:

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:44.160
"Michael was my first and&nbsp;
only choice for the role."

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:48.800
Wright noted in the same CinemaBlend&nbsp;
interview that he and co-writer Michael Bacall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:53.920
first got the idea to cast Cera when they saw him&nbsp;
in the acclaimed sitcom "Arrested Development."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:58.320
They only wished he was older at the time, but&nbsp;
as fate would have it, it took five years to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:02.880
"Scott Pilgrim" made. By the time casting&nbsp;
rolled around, Cera was the perfect age.

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:05.920
"Hi, I was thinking about asking you out but&nbsp;
then I realized how stupid that would be.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:07.600
So do you want to go out some time?"

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.880
Wright also noted that another advantage to&nbsp;
casting Cera in the part was the ability to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:18.000
contrast the film's intense action sequences with&nbsp;
the look and mannerisms of someone like Cera.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.400
Plus, the young actor was Canadian, further&nbsp;
cementing just how right he was for the part.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:25.920
 
Edgar Wright movies often have tons of memorable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:31.040
supporting performances, with the likes of "Baby&nbsp;
Driver" or "Hot Fuzz" being two-character stories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:35.920
chock full of unforgettable supporting figures.&nbsp;
"Scott Pilgrim vs. the World," meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:40.880
has the most packed cast of any Wright feature,&nbsp;
with many of the characters developed far beyond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:45.840
just one-dimensional stereotypes. Working with&nbsp;
so many performers at once could intimidate some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:50.640
filmmakers, but Wright embraced the chance&nbsp;
to work with such a stacked ensemble cast.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:53.680
As he explained to CinemaBlend,&nbsp;
one of Wright's favorite things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:57.840
about the film is the way you can watch&nbsp;
supporting players in every single shot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:01.840
and see a different version of the&nbsp;
story unfolding. As Wright put it:

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:06.160
"That's very true to the books. In the books,&nbsp;
there's this kind of peanut gallery feel where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:10.160
there's things going on and nothing going on&nbsp;
in the background, and the ensemble is always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:13.440
doing something in the books. I tried&nbsp;
to pull that off in the film as well."

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:19.680
"Scott Pilgrim vs. the World" is a departure from&nbsp;
other Edgar Wright movies in several key ways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:24.000
including that the project had a significantly&nbsp;
bigger budget than prior Wright works like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.920
"Shaun of the Dead." However, another key&nbsp;
departure from Wright's other films was that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:32.800
this British-born filmmaker was insistent that&nbsp;
there were no British performers in the cast.

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:36.080
"You want to fight me...for her?"

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:40.320
"Was that not clear? Was that not clear?"

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:45.040
Of course, European talent is so widespread&nbsp;
in the acting community that someone from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:49.680
across the pond was bound to sneak onto the&nbsp;
cast list for "Scott Pilgrim vs. the World."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:53.840
Satya Bhabha, who played Matthew Patel&nbsp;
in the film, was not just born in London,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:58.880
but grew up in the same part of London as Wright&nbsp;
himself. Bhabha's years working in America were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:02.640
enough to help him keep Wright unaware of&nbsp;
his history during the casting process.

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:07.760
One of the performances most critical to&nbsp;
making "Scott Pilgrim vs. The World" work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:12.720
as well as it does is Ellen Wong's turn as Knives&nbsp;
Chau. What could have been a passive performance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:17.600
the forgettable "other girl" to the more prominent&nbsp;
Ramona Flowers, becomes unforgettable in Wong's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:22.480
hands. What's especially impressive is that Wong&nbsp;
makes sure Chau never lapses into being a mocking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:27.280
caricature. Even when she's having a breakdown&nbsp;
over Scott Pilgrim seeing another, older woman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:31.440
Wong injects a relatable quality into&nbsp;
Chau's character that makes us fully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:35.360
understand her perspective. Given just&nbsp;
how perfectly she inhabited this role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:37.600
it can be shocking to hear that, initially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:42.400
Wong was convinced she had no chance at securing&nbsp;
the part. As she told Entertainment Weekly:

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:45.760
"As an Asian actress, I was never&nbsp;
going out for a role like this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:49.120
This was not the type of role that&nbsp;
I would be able to even look at."

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:51.360
Thankfully, for the sake of the overall movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:56.480
Wong not only auditioned for "Scott Pilgrim vs.&nbsp;
the World" but also scored the part of Knives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:00.480
in the process ensuring this film would&nbsp;
have one of its most important performances.

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:06.240
"Scott Pilgrim vs. the World" has garnered varying&nbsp;
degrees of responses to its approach regarding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:11.040
female characters. However, one objectively true&nbsp;
fact about the film is that there are several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:15.840
women in the story beyond Scott Pilgrim's love&nbsp;
interests Ramona Flowers and Knives Chau. Played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:20.400
by the likes of Brie Larson and Aubrey Plaza,&nbsp;
among others, these characters and their comedic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:25.120
moments are some of Edgar Wright's favorite&nbsp;
parts of this production. As he told Collider:

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:28.800
"The thing I'm really pleased about with this&nbsp;
film, as opposed to some of the other comedies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:32.800
out there — and even stuff I've done before&nbsp;
— is that it has a lot of funny women in it."

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:36.560
Given how the characters played by performers&nbsp;
like Plaza and Larson became some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:39.840
most beloved scene-stealers in&nbsp;
"Scott Pilgrim vs. the World,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:43.680
it's a good thing Wright made time to shine&nbsp;
the spotlight on multiple women in the film.

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:48.960
While a widely acclaimed project, "Scott&nbsp;
Pilgrim vs. the World" has, like any movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:53.600
not been without some criticism. Most notably,&nbsp;
the film has been critiqued for its casting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:57.840
which largely employed white actors. Among&nbsp;
those who have registered grievances with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:02.400
this is Bryan Lee O'Malley, who penned the&nbsp;
film's source graphic novels. An author of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:07.120
French-Canadian and Korean heritage, O'Malley&nbsp;
has been open about his issues with the eventual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:11.840
casting of the "Scott Pilgrim vs. the World"&nbsp;
movie. In a post on his blog, Radio Maru:

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:15.600
"Honestly, when I saw the Scott Pilgrim&nbsp;
movie it was kind of appalling to see just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:19.920
how white it was — to not even really&nbsp;
see myself represented on the screen."

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:23.600
He went on to note that the casting of this&nbsp;
film was a key influence in his decision to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:28.720
add more diversity to his subsequent works,&nbsp;
including books like "Seconds." These comments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:33.520
reflect the complicated legacy of even movies as&nbsp;
well-received as "Scott Pilgrim vs. the World."

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:39.040
American movies filmed in Canada are a dime&nbsp;
a dozen, thanks to the country's generous tax&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:44.480
incentives. However, these productions usually&nbsp;
use Canadian backdrops to double as other places,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:49.760
including famous American cities. "Scott Pilgrim&nbsp;
vs. the World" departed from this norm by taking a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:54.720
cue from its source material and setting Scott's&nbsp;
story in specific locations all across Toronto,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:59.280
Canada. To make sure he approached these&nbsp;
backdrops properly, Edgar Wright did his homework&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:04.000
and committed fully to understanding just where&nbsp;
he'd be shooting "Scott Pilgrim vs. The World."

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:06.800
The Globe and Mail noted that&nbsp;
"Scott Pilgrim vs. the World"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:10.240
was one of the biggest films to be&nbsp;
set in Toronto up to that point.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:14.800
This feat was accompanied by Wright spending&nbsp;
countless hours observing the terrain of Toronto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:19.280
and making sure "Scott Pilgrim" would utilize&nbsp;
landmarks in the area. Bryan Lee O'Malley's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:24.000
graphic novels are extremely specific in terms&nbsp;
of where in Toronto they take place, and Wright&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:28.640
made a commitment to recreate that with the film.&nbsp;
Committing to this backdrop is emblematic of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:33.840
specific details that make "Scott Pilgrim vs. the&nbsp;
World" such a uniquely entertaining experience.

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:37.520
The hardest action scene
The assorted action sequences in "Scott Pilgrim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:43.280
vs. the World" are a masterful blend of visual and&nbsp;
auditory elements that required a lot of effort to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:47.760
execute. But one particular action scene turned&nbsp;
out to be the most challenging part of "Scott&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:52.640
Pilgrim vs. the World" to film. This was none&nbsp;
other than the climactic showdown between Scott&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:57.120
Pilgrim and Gideon Graves. As Wright recalled&nbsp;
in an interview with Entertainment Weekly:

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:00.960
"And then there was this final set piece&nbsp;
on a pyramid. Suddenly, we slowed down to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:05.760
doing 10 shots in a day, which is very slow&nbsp;
for me. I sat there on top of this pyramid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:09.440
looking down at these enormous platforms&nbsp;
being maneuvered around and I thought,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:11.840
'Whose idea was this?' And it was mine."

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:16.640
While the scene itself proved to be exhilarating&nbsp;
to watch unfold in a movie theater, it's apparent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:21.440
the complicated sets and choreography made&nbsp;
it an enormous undertaking to actually shoot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:25.120
Thankfully, for Wright and company, all of&nbsp;
that effort ended up paying off handsomely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:29.840
since this climactic battle closed out "Scott&nbsp;
Pilgrim vs. the World" on an exciting high note.

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:34.240
From the very start, "Scott Pilgrim vs.&nbsp;
the World" makes it apparent you're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:38.400
to watch something special when a performance&nbsp;
from the band Sex Bob-omb triggers a visually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:42.480
aggressive opening credits sequence. The&nbsp;
names of key cast and crew members are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:46.560
displayed against frantic animation and&nbsp;
a wave of colors that clue the viewer in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:50.960
on the kind of visually unpredictable and&nbsp;
audacious movie they're about to absorb.

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:55.440
In breaking this sequence down for The Art of&nbsp;
the Title Sequence, Edgar Wright revealed several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.440 --> 00:08:00.320
fascinating facets of this segment, including&nbsp;
that its very existence came at the suggestion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:05.040
of Quentin Tarantino after the "Pulp Fiction"&nbsp;
director saw an early screening of the film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:08.560
The artists in charge of crafting these&nbsp;
opening credits also had to come up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:12.720
their own unique visuals — they couldn't draw&nbsp;
from imagery in either the film itself or the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:16.800
"Scott Pilgrim" graphic novels. It took&nbsp;
an enormous amount of effort and craft to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:21.040
pull off such an unusual approach to an opening&nbsp;
credits sequence, but the result was something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:25.840
that made "Scott Pilgrim vs. the World" a&nbsp;
captivating experience right from the start.

00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:30.160
Music plays a big part in "Scott Pilgrim&nbsp;
vs. the World." How could it not?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:34.480
After all, Scott plays in a band called Sex&nbsp;
Bob-omb and so many of the film's big action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:40.000
scenes begin with bands performing on stage.&nbsp;
But music's influence in this film was not just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:45.040
limited to in-universe performances. The world&nbsp;
of musicals also had a significant influence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:50.400
on how Edgar Wright composed the various major&nbsp;
action set pieces. As the director told Collider:

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:52.800
"We thought it should play&nbsp;
out like a musical in a way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:56.480
in terms of the fights are not dissimilar&nbsp;
to the songs. I always thought there were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.480 --> 00:09:00.960
a lot of martial arts films that were like&nbsp;
musicals, so we wanted to take that further."

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:05.120
You see the results onscreen, as many of the&nbsp;
film's fight scenes play out like elaborate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.120 --> 00:09:09.040
dance numbers. It was a style Wright&nbsp;
would take a step further years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.040 --> 00:09:12.000
with his musically inspired&nbsp;
car chase film "Baby Driver."

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:17.360
In the aftermath of "Scott Pilgrim vs. The&nbsp;
World" failing to take the box office by storm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:21.840
the conversation inevitably turned to what&nbsp;
went wrong. One recurring answer as to how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:26.240
such an acclaimed film failed to click with&nbsp;
audiences was to look at the film's marketing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:31.600
which many deemed lackluster and a key culprit in&nbsp;
the box office performance. Of course, those who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:36.160
made the film have their own thoughts as to what&nbsp;
it was like to advertise such an unorthodox title.

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:41.280
Speaking to Entertainment Weekly for the film's&nbsp;
10th anniversary in 2020, Wright opened up about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:46.000
the disappointment, noting how tempting it was to&nbsp;
blame other people for the failure. The director&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:50.160
has been asked numerous times over the years&nbsp;
what the key factor was for the film's box office&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:54.880
disappointment, but he's always been resistant&nbsp;
to the idea that just one thing was responsible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.440 --> 00:10:00.480
In the end, Wright just seemed glad that the&nbsp;
film found new life on home video. As he told EW:

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:05.120
"After the film didn't open well, I realized&nbsp;
the thing to do is to take responsibility for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:09.920
it but also just believe in the fact that it was&nbsp;
a good film and people would find it eventually."

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:12.560
And people did indeed find it eventually. Today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:18.080
"Scott Pilgrim vs. The World" is considered&nbsp;
one of the great cult classics of the 2010s.

